<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#luganda">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Luganda</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ganda language</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Luganda is the Bantu language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baganda">Baganda</a> of central Uganda. It is spoken as a first language by several million people and as a second language by many more, making it the most widely spoken indigenous language of Uganda. It was written in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> alphabet by missionaries from the 1870s, and a substantial literature, including histories of Buganda's kings, appeared from around 1900.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Luganda, called Oluganda by its speakers, is a Bantu language of the African Great Lakes region. It is agglutinative and tonal, with subject-verb-object word order. It has at least 5.6 million first-language speakers, mainly in the Buganda region of central Uganda, including the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kampala">Kampala</a>, and about 5.4 million second-language speakers in towns such as Mbale, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinja">Jinja</a>, Gulu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbarara">Mbarara</a>, where it is used mostly in trade. As a second language in Uganda it follows English and precedes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a>. It is also the most spoken unofficial language in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kigali">Kigali</a>, the capital of Rwanda. Lusoga, spoken in Busoga to the east of Buganda, is very closely related, with an estimated lexical similarity of 82 to 86 per cent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Like the other Bantu languages of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a> region, Luganda is generally held to descend from Proto-Bantu, the ancestor of all Bantu languages, though its specific origins remain a matter of scholarly debate. During the 18th and 19th centuries contact with Arab traders, missionaries and colonial powers brought loanwords, mostly for things that had not existed in the country. From Arabic come ccaayi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a>, from shay, as well as eddiini, religion, and essaala, prayer; from English come essaati, shirt, and emotoka, car.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Luganda distinguishes long from short vowels and has geminate consonants, which speakers call simply doubling, as in bbiri, two. It has three tones, high, low and falling, and pitch changes meaning: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabaka-of-buganda">Kabaka</a> means king when all three syllables have the same pitch, but with a high first syllable it means the little one catches.</p><p>Spelling, standardised since 1947, uses the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> alphabet with one added letter, ŋ, and the digraph ny, counted as a single letter. Letters and sounds correspond closely. Its grammar centres on nouns: adjectives, verbs, pronouns and possessives agree with a noun by class and number through prefixes. Ten classes are usually counted, plus four locative classes. Nouns also have two states, with the topic state marked by an initial vowel, as in the subject of a sentence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Luganda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
